      "math_academy_id": "q-149717",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Find the value of the double sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}({a}_{i}+1)({b}_{j}-1)\n$$\n\ngiven that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{a}_{i}=20,\\,{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}{b}_{j}=26.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The product rule for double summations states that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{b}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{a}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}{b}_{j}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the product rule for double summations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}({a}_{i}+1)({b}_{j}-1) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}({a}_{i}+1){\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}({b}_{j}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the rules for single summations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}({a}_{i}+1){\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}({b}_{j}-1) & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{a}_{i}+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1)\\cdot ({\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}{b}_{j}-{\\sum }_{j=1}^{20}1) \\\\  & =(20+5)\\cdot (26-20) \\\\  & =(25)\\cdot (6) \\\\  & =150.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
